  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through next Monday)\nIssued at 205 PM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nThe weak frontal boundary will progress further south on Thursday, \nkeeping temperatures cooler in the 80s & 90s while likely settling \nsouth of the I-40 corridor between Amarillo and Lubbock. In the \nupper levels, a lead shortwave is projected to cross over the NE \nPanhandles into western OK, with a secondary disturbance within \nnorthwest flow trailing further west across the region. The lead \nshortwave will be responsible for early morning convection across \nprimarily the northern and eastern Panhandles, that should gradually \ndie off later in the day once upper lift departs. Isolated to \nscattered afternoon-evening convection could then develop with \neither of the aforementioned disturbances, but CAMs are split on \nwhich system is more likely to produce anything. \n\nThe southern and eastern Texas Panhandle would hypothetically be \nmost favored for storms, with closer proximity to the boundary \nand lead shortwave (20-50% POPs). However, if the lead wave is too\nfar removed to our east, subsidence could keep much of the area \nsubdued. Then, our attention would shift to the western Panhandles\nwhere several models hint that the trailing disturbance could \ninitiate scattered storms through the evening hours (20-40% POPs).\nRegardless, CAPE and shear values could be even more favorable \nfor severe storms Thursday, with 1000-2000 J/kg MUCAPE and 25-40kt\n0-6km shear being forecast. There are concerns with mid-level\ncapping inversions seen in forecast soundings causing convection\nto struggle, but this should not completely negate a severe\nthreat. Damaging winds, large hail, and localized flooding from \ntorrential rainfall would be the primary hazards. \n\nOur active pattern takes a break Friday, as we unfortunately return \nto near triple digit heat. Most models project high pressure staying \nshunted just south of the Panhandles to end the week into next week, \nwhich will allow weak fronts to reach the area Saturday, and again \nnext Mon-Tue. Daily temperatures are forecast to gradually trend \ncooler as a result, possibly even briefly returning near seasonal \naverages in the 80s mid next week. Ample moisture should stick \naround the region as well, meaning more precipitation opportunities \ncould be in the cards with any front and synoptic system.\n\nHarrel",
